'' AN 'iif' HAS NO TYPE AT ALL -- Check computes none for one, deliberately, because an
'' arm may be an intrinsic with no type either, and every wide and narrow path is
'' STRUCTURAL about it instead. So 'dim a as U = iif( c, wstr( "a" ), u2 )' reached the
'' converting-constructor path with nothing to pick a constructor by, and declined.
''
'' The CONSTRUCTOR is what supplies the missing type: a one-argument converting
'' constructor over a zstring or wstring POINTER names the width, and the argument
'' lowering is structural about iif in both. Measured, fbc builds both arms into ONE
'' wide temporary and hands it to the constructor:
''
''   vr$3 = fb_WstrAlloc( fb_WstrLen( L"a" ) ); TMP$6 = vr$3; fb_WstrAssign( TMP$6, 0, L"a" )
''   vr$6 = _ZN14UWSTRING_FIXEDcvwEv( &U2$1 ); ... fb_WstrAssign( TMP$6, 0, TMP$7 )
''   _ZN14UWSTRING_FIXEDC1EPKw( &A$1, TMP$6 ); fb_WstrDelete( TMP$6 )
''
'' -- which is the shape the wide iif already emitted; only the way in was missing.
'' udt-wstring/iif.bas: 10 -> 0.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/udt-iif-ctor.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' EXPECT-OUT: fb_WstrAlloc
'' GOLDEN: expected/udt-iif-ctor.out
